approvals instead of the generic spec-before-code gate of the
`graph-agents-cli-workflow` skill, and treat the generic gate as satisfied only
by that process's approvals. When it is `null`, the generic gate applies:
write `.graph-agents-cli-spec.md` and get it approved before writing code.

## The loop

1. Build in `{{cookiecutter.agent_directory}}/`. Tools live in `{{cookiecutter.agent_directory}}/tools/`; every tool module
   (subpackages included) declares `API_CALLS` as one literal list (empty when it calls no
   external API) and reaches an external API only through `get_client("<api>")` of
   `{{cookiecutter.agent_directory}}/app_utils/api_client.py`, which enforces `api-policy.yaml` and fails closed.
   Keep `graph` unbound (no checkpointer) and keep its `recursion_limit` config and
   `SurfaceApiErrors` middleware.
2. Try it: `cp .env.example .env` and `graph-agents-cli login --write-env` once, then
   `graph-agents-cli playground` (the app with `/playground`), or `graph-agents-cli run "prompt"`. `graph-agents-cli playground --graph` opens
   LangGraph Studio against the graph directly (bypasses the auth policy).
3. Write eval cases by hand in `tests/eval/datasets/`, then `graph-agents-cli eval run`
   until the gate passes. `eval compare` shows regressions; `eval analyze` clusters failures.
4. `uv run pytest` and `graph-agents-cli lint` before deploying.
